The Primera Ordre de Balboa (POB; First Order of Balboa) was a white nationalist terrorist organisation active in the Balboan interior from the late 1960s to 1976. Founded by [[Francesc Alemany i Bosch]], the son of a wealthy Tramuntana planter who had been compensated for the expropriation of his estate under the [[Twelve Guarantees]] land redistribution programme, the POB sought to reverse the Guarantees, restore the planter oligarchy's political and economic dominance, and re-establish the racial hierarchy of the [[Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|rump state]].

The organisation began as a political movement holding rallies in cities across [[Nurra Province|Nurra]] and [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]], but was forced underground after orchestrating the [[1970 Novara race riots]]. As funding dwindled, the POB turned to drug manufacturing in the highlands, establishing a headquarters in [[Altbirú]] before relocating to the [[La Guàrdia (comarca)|La Guàrdia comarca]] after violent clashes with local [[Biru people|Biru]] residents. Following the [[Siege of Murado Ranch]] in 1974, the POB escalated to open terrorism — conducting a campaign of kidnappings, killings, and robberies targeting Biru and non-white Balboans, culminating in the [[Sant Bart University bombing]] in February 1975, the deadliest terrorist attack in Balboan history. The organisation was destroyed in the [[La Guàrdia Campaign]] of 1975–1976, in which Alemany i Bosch and the POB's senior leadership were killed and its remaining members were arrested or dispersed.

The POB inspired the [[Segon Ordre de Balboa]] (Second Order of Balboa), a loosely organised white nationalist group that emerged on the internet in the 2000s, though the two organisations have no direct organisational continuity.

== Background and formation ==

{{main|Twelve Guarantees|Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)}}

The [[Twelve Guarantees]], accepted by the Balboan government in 1960 as a condition for the return of the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Sant Cristòfor mandate]], required the dismantling of the racial state that had defined the [[Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|rump state]] since 1899. The Guarantees mandated full civil rights, anti-discrimination protections, and reparations to descendants of enslaved persons, including the redistribution of land from the white planter oligarchy to the Black and mixed Balboan population. The [[Balboan Land and Reparations Authority]] (BLRA), established in 1962, began processing expropriations in the mandate territory during the integration era and expanded into the interior provinces after reunification in 1973.

The planter class's response to the Guarantees was divided. Most planters accepted the expropriation process — reluctantly, in many cases — and took the compensation offered by the BLRA, which was payable in government bonds and, in some cases, cash. A minority, however, regarded the Guarantees as an unconstitutional infringement on property rights and the land redistribution as the dispossession of their class. Among this minority was [[Francesc Alemany i Bosch]], the son of a wealthy Tramuntana planter whose estate had been expropriated by the BLRA in the mid-1960s.

=== Francesc Alemany i Bosch ===

[[Francesc Alemany i Bosch]] (1944–1976) was born into one of the established planter families of Tramuntana. His father, [[Lluís Alemany i Sala]], owned a large sugar plantation on the Tramuntana savanna that had been in the family for four generations. The BLRA expropriated the estate in 1965 and offered compensation at its assessed valuation, payable in government bonds. Lluís Alemany i Sala accepted the compensation — which was, by the standards of the programme, fair — and relocated to [[Portblanc]], where he lived comfortably on the proceeds. By the standards of the redistribution, the Alemany family was left "pretty fine": the father had been paid out, the family retained its social standing in the white community, and the financial impact of the expropriation was cushioned by the compensation.

Francesc Alemany i Bosch, however, did not accept the expropriation as his father had. He had been a student at the [[Universitat de Sant Bart]] when the Guarantees were implemented and had been radicalised by the sight of the planter class's political displacement. He regarded his father's acceptance of the compensation as a betrayal — not because the family had been impoverished, but because the expropriation itself was, in his view, an illegitimate act of state theft. Alemany i Bosch's ideology was not primarily economic: the Alemany family had not been ruined, and Alemany i Bosch himself had access to the compensation funds. His motivation was ideological and racial: he regarded the Guarantees as the destruction of the natural order of Balboan society and the planter class's political displacement as an injustice that justified violent resistance.

== Early activities and the 1970 Novara race riots ==

Alemany i Bosch began organising in the late 1960s, holding rallies in cities across Tramuntana and Nurra — particularly in [[Portblanc]], [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]], and the smaller towns of the Tramuntana savanna. The rallies attracted audiences from the white planter community and from the white working class in the interior cities, who were resentful of the economic dislocation produced by the integration process and the racial integration that the Guarantees mandated. Alemany i Bosch was a charismatic speaker who combined the planter class's grievance against the land redistribution with a broader racial narrative that cast the Guarantees as an assault on white Balboan identity.

The organisation that Alemany i Bosch built around these rallies was the Primera Ordre de Balboa — the "First Order," a name that invoked the colonial-era planter order and implied that the rump state's racial hierarchy was the original and legitimate organisation of Balboan society. The POB's early activities were primarily political: rallies, pamphlets, and the formation of local chapters in Tramuntana and Nurra. But the organisation also began stockpiling weapons — primarily hunting rifles and shotguns, but also a small number of military-grade firearms obtained through the constabulary networks that remained sympathetic to the planter class — and conducting paramilitary training on private land in the Tramuntana highlands.

=== The 1970 Novara race riots ===

The POB's transition from political organisation to violent actor came with the [[1970 Novara race riots]], a series of attacks on Black and mixed Balboan residents of [[Novara]], [[Nurra Province]], on 1 July 1970. The riots were triggered by a false story, published in Novara's main newspaper through the POB's connections with the paper's editorial staff, that a white woman had been attacked and raped by a gang of Black men hiding in a Black neighbourhood. The story was fabricated, and it was deliberately published on [[Emancipation Day]] — the anniversary of the abolition of slavery in Balboa on 1 July 1848 — which Black Balboans observed as a holy day of rest, remembrance, and church services. The timing was calculated: the Black community was at home for the holiday, while the white community was not, and the POB's supporters were free to mobilise. The story spread rapidly through Novara's white community and produced the outrage that the POB needed. POB members and sympathisers, deployed to Novara from surrounding towns, used the fabricated story as a pretext for a series of coordinated attacks on Black-owned businesses, the homes of Black families who had settled in Novara under the Guarantees' anti-discrimination provisions, and the integrated public schools that had been the subject of the white community's resentment.

The riots produced several days of violence, numerous injuries, and significant property damage before the constabulary restored order. The investigation that followed identified the POB's role in orchestrating the attacks, and the organisation was banned by the Balboan government in late 1970. The ban forced the POB underground and marked the end of its open political activities.

== Underground and the drug trade ==

The ban and the subsequent scrutiny from the constabulary and the [[International Cooperative Police Force]] (ICPF) forced the POB to restructure. Alemany i Bosch and the organisation's leadership went underground, and the POB's funding — which had depended on donations from sympathetic planters and on Alemany i Bosch's own access to his family's compensation funds — began to dwindle. The organisation's stockpiling of weapons required ongoing expenditure, and the cost of maintaining a clandestine network of members and safe houses was substantial.

To finance its operations, the POB turned to drug manufacturing. The organisation's relocation to the highlands of [[Jala Province|Jala]] and Tramuntana — areas where the state's authority was weak and the terrain was remote — provided the conditions for clandestine drug laboratories. The POB's members, drawn from the planter class, had connections to the agricultural economy and the knowledge of chemistry and logistics needed to establish manufacturing operations. The drugs were distributed through criminal gangs in the coastal cities — particularly in [[Sant Bart]] and [[Portblanc]] — who provided the distribution networks and the port access through which the drugs could be concealed in legitimate agricultural exports.

The POB established its first clandestine headquarters in [[Altbirú]], a remote highland settlement in Jala Province. The location offered isolation and defensible terrain, but the POB's presence brought it into conflict with the local [[Biru people|Biru]] population, who regarded the white nationalist organisation's occupation of their territory as a continuation of the dispossession they had experienced under the planter oligarchy. The clashes between the POB and Biru community defence groups escalated through the early 1970s, and the POB was eventually forced to relocate from Altbirú to the [[La Guàrdia (comarca)|La Guàrdia comarca]], a more remote highland area where the organisation established its primary base of operations.

== La Guàrdia and the escalation ==

The POB's relocation to La Guàrdia coincided with the reunification of 1973 and the acceleration of the land redistribution programme in the interior provinces. The [[Siege of Murado Ranch]] in March 1974 — in which the Alvarado administration deployed the military to enforce the expropriation of the Murado family's plantation — was perceived by Alemany i Bosch as a declaration of war against the planter class. The siege demonstrated that the federal government was willing to use military force to enforce the Guarantees, and Alemany i Bosch concluded that the POB needed to escalate from guerrilla harassment to a spectacular act of terrorism that would demonstrate the organisation's capacity to strike at the heart of the post-reunification order.

In the period between the Murado Ranch siege and the Sant Bart University bombing, the POB conducted a campaign of violence in the highlands that targeted Biru and non-white Balboans. POB operatives kidnapped and killed random Biru individuals and non-white travellers in the highlands, drove through Biru communities terrorising residents, and stole money and property to fund the organisation's operations. The campaign was not strategically sophisticated: it was a campaign of terror designed to intimidate the non-white population of the highlands and to demonstrate the state's inability to protect them. The violence produced a significant death toll — the exact number is unknown, as many of the victims were remote highland residents whose disappearances were not reported to the authorities — and contributed to the displacement of Biru communities from the areas around the POB's La Guàrdia base.

The campaign also served a practical purpose: the robberies and thefts provided the funding that the POB's dwindling donations and drug operations could no longer supply, and the kidnappings provided a source of ransom income. The POB's violence was, in effect, a self-sustaining criminal enterprise that financed its political terrorism.

== The Sant Bart University bombing ==

{{main|Sant Bart University bombing}}

The POB's escalation culminated in the [[Sant Bart University bombing]] on 12 February 1975. A car bomb detonated outside the Faculty of Law building at the [[Universitat de Sant Bart]] during morning lectures killed 42 people — mostly Black and mixed students and faculty — and injured 78 others. The bombing was the deadliest terrorist attack in Balboan history and was intended as retaliation for the government's enforcement of the land redistribution programme and as a warning against the further integration of the interior provinces.

The bombing was planned and authorised by Alemany i Bosch and the POB's senior leadership at the La Guàrdia base. The operatives who carried out the attack — [[Joan Ballester i Font]], [[Rafel Soler i Mas]], and [[Pere Vidal i Renom]] — were POB members who had been selected for their ability to operate in Sant Bart without attracting suspicion. The bomb was constructed using explosives expertise available within the POB's network, and the logistics were coordinated through the organisation's criminal connections in the coastal cities.

The bombing was not the POB's only targeted killing in this period. In May 1975, the organisation assassinated [[Deputy mayor of Sant Bart|deputy mayor of Sant Bart]] [[Bartomeu Riera i Solà]], a Black Balboan BPP politician who had been a vocal advocate for the Guarantees and the land redistribution programme. Riera i Solà was shot outside his home in Sant Bart by a POB operative; the assassination was intended to demonstrate that the organisation could reach prominent political figures and to intimidate BPP officials in the interior provinces.

== The La Guàrdia Campaign and the destruction of the POB ==

{{main|La Guàrdia Campaign}}

The Sant Bart University bombing triggered the [[La Guàrdia Campaign]] (1975–1976), a series of military and police incursions into the highlands of Jala Province to root out the POB's training camps and safe havens. The campaign, named after the [[La Guàrdia (comarca)|La Guàrdia comarca]] where the POB had established its primary base, was the largest domestic military operation in Balboan history.

The campaign involved thousands of troops and police officers deployed to root out terrorist cells, seize weapons, and dismantle the logistical networks that supported the POB. The military conducted sustained incursions into the highland areas around La Guàrdia, engaging the POB's armed cells in a series of firefights and sieges. Alemany i Bosch and the POB's senior leadership were killed in a siege of the organisation's La Guàrdia headquarters in late 1975. The remaining POB members were arrested in a series of raids through early 1976 or dispersed into the highlands, where some were later linked to the independent drug operations that supplanted the POB's drug manufacturing network.

The La Guàrdia Campaign also produced the controversial policy of granting special arms licenses to [[Biru people|Biru]] communities in the highlands to defend themselves against white nationalist cells — a programme that would have unintended consequences, including the 1977 [[Aeroístmus hijacking]] by the [[Biru Freedom Movement]].

== Legacy ==

The Primera Ordre de Balboa was destroyed as an organisation by the La Guàrdia Campaign, but its legacy persisted in two forms: the highland drug trade, which had been established by the POB and was subsequently taken over by Biru groups and then independent operators, and the white nationalist ideological current that the POB had articulated.

=== Segon Ordre de Balboa ===

{{main|Segon Ordre de Balboa}}

The [[Segon Ordre de Balboa]] (Second Order of Balboa) is a loosely organised white nationalist group that emerged on the internet in the 2000s. The group takes its name and its ideological inspiration from the POB, but it has no direct organisational continuity with the original organisation. The Segon Ordre operates primarily through online forums and social media, and its membership is diffuse and geographically scattered, lacking the organisational structure, the territorial base, or the operational capacity of the POB. The group's activities have consisted primarily of online propaganda, occasional public demonstrations, and isolated incidents of violence by individuals inspired by its ideology. The Balboan security services have identified the Segon Ordre as a domestic extremist threat, but the group's decentralised structure has made it difficult to monitor or suppress.

